            <ArticleTitle>Can mean platelet volume and red blood cell distribution width show disease activity in rheumatoid arthritis?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>967-74</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/bmm-2016-0148</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the relationship between mean platelet volume (MPV) and red cell distribution width (RDW), and disease activity in rheumatoid arthritis (RA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS &amp; METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Hundred RA and 100 controls were included.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">MPV and RDW were higher in RA (p &lt; 0.0001). The cut-off levels of RDW and MPV were 14.8 and 10.4. Patients with RDW &gt;14.8 had higher Disease Activity Score 28 (DAS28; p = 0.002) and pain score (p = 0.0007). RDW was positively correlated with DAS28 and pain. But, DAS28 and pain were not different between patients with MPV &gt;10.4 and &lt;10.4.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">MPV and RDW were significantly higher in RA. RDW and MPV were similar to erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein to indicate inflammatory activity. RDW was correlated with pain and DAS28, but MPV was not associated with them.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
